{"global_index": "MMLU_formal_logic_61", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Identify the conclusion of the following argument. There are those who use predicate variables; the values of these variables are attributes. Predicates are wanted in all sentences regardless of whether there are attributes to refer to. The dummy predicate 'F' is wanted generally for expository purposes. If we are going to quantify over attributes and refer to them, then clarity is served by using recognizable variables and distinctive names for the purposes, and not mixing these up with the predicates. (Quine, Philosophy of Logic)\n\nOptions: \nA. There are those who use predicate variables; the values of these variables are attributes.\nB. Predicates are wanted in all sentences regardless of whether there are attributes to refer to.\nC. The dummy predicate 'F' is wanted generally for expository purposes.\nD. If we are going to quantify over attributes and refer to them, then clarity is served by using recognizable variables and distinctive names for the purposes, and not mixing these up with the predicates.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{D}. This option encapsulates the main conclusion of the argument, emphasizing the importance of clarity in distinguishing between variables, names, and predicates when quantifying over attributes. The preceding statements support this conclusion by setting up the context and reasoning behind it.", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 258, "output_tokens": 56, "total_tokens": 314}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{D}. This option encapsulates the main conclusion of the argument, emphasizing the importance of clarity in distinguishing between variables, names, and predicates when quantifying over attributes. The preceding statements support this conclusion by setting up the context and reasoning behind it.", "ground_truth": "3", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 7.23e-05}}
{"global_index": "MMLU_formal_logic_69", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Select the best English interpretation of the given arguments in predicate logic.\n(∃x)(Cx • Ox)\n(∀x)[(~Cx ⊃ ~Bx) ⊃ ~Og]\t/ ~Og\n\nOptions: \nA. Some cookies have oatmeal. If something's not being a cookie entails that it doesn't have chocolate chips, then this cookie doesn't have oatmeal. So this cookie doesn't have oatmeal.\nB. Some cookies have oatmeal. If something is not a cookie and does not have chocolate chips, it doesn't have oatmeal. So this cookie doesn't have oatmeal.\nC. Some cookies have oatmeal. If something doesn't have oatmeal, then it is not a cookie and it doesn't have chocolate chips. So this cookie doesn't have oatmeal.\nD. Some cookies have oatmeal. If something's not having chocolate chips entails that it is not a cookie, then it doesn't have oatmeal. So this cookie doesn't have oatmeal.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).\n\nThis option accurately captures the predicates and implications of the logical expressions, stating that if something’s not being a cookie entails that it doesn’t have chocolate chips, then the conclusion follows that this particular cookie does not have oatmeal. The phrasing aligns well with the structure and meanings of the predicates in the original logical statements.", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 247, "output_tokens": 74, "total_tokens": 321}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).\n\nThis option accurately captures the predicates and implications of the logical expressions, stating that if something’s not being a cookie entails that it doesn’t have chocolate chips, then the conclusion follows that this particular cookie does not have oatmeal. The phrasing aligns well with the structure and meanings of the predicates in the original logical statements.", "ground_truth": "0", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 8.145e-05}}
{"global_index": "MMLU_formal_logic_49", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Select the best English interpretation of the given arguments in predicate logic.\nDh ⊃ ~Pt\n(∀x)Px ∨ (∀x)Mx\n~Mb\t/ ~Dh\n\nOptions: \nA. If my headache is dualist state, then your tickle is a physical state. Either everything is physical or everything is mental. But my broken toe is not a mental state. So my headache is not a dualist state.\nB. If my headache is dualist state, then your tickle is not a physical state. Either everything is physical or everything is mental. But my broken toe is not a mental state. So my headache is not a dualist state.\nC. If my headache is dualist state, then your tickle is not a physical state. If everything is physical then everything is mental. But my broken toe is not a mental state. So my headache is not a dualist state.\nD. If my headache is dualist state, then your tickle is not a physical state. Everything is either physical or mental. But my broken toe is not a mental state. So my headache is not a dualist state.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "The best interpretation of the given arguments in predicate logic is option D. The key components of the original expressions are correctly interpreted: Dh ⊃ ~Pt suggests a conditional about dualist headaches and physical tickles, and the overall statement regarding everything being either physical or mental aligns well with \"Everything is either physical or mental.\" Therefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{D}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 299, "output_tokens": 77, "total_tokens": 376}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "The best interpretation of the given arguments in predicate logic is option D. The key components of the original expressions are correctly interpreted: Dh ⊃ ~Pt suggests a conditional about dualist headaches and physical tickles, and the overall statement regarding everything being either physical or mental aligns well with \"Everything is either physical or mental.\" Therefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{D}\\).", "ground_truth": "1", "score": 0.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 9.104999999999999e-05}}
{"global_index": "MMLU_formal_logic_101", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Select the best English interpretation of the given arguments in predicate logic.\nWn ∨ Wm\n(∀x)[Lx ⊃ (Dx ⊃ ~Wx)]\nLn • Dn\t/ ~(∀x)~Wx\n\nOptions: \nA. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is not at work.\nB. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are out to dinner if they are not at work. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is not at work.\nC. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are out to dinner if they are not at work. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is at work.\nD. Either Nancy or Marvin are at work. All lawyers are not at work if they are out to dinner. Nancy is a lawyer and out to dinner. So not everything is at work.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{D}\\). This option accurately reflects the original arguments: it indicates that either Nancy or Marvin is at work, clarifies the conditional relationship regarding lawyers and dinner, asserts Nancy's status, and properly concludes that not everything is at work. The phrasing aligns well with predicate logic interpretations.", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 274, "output_tokens": 65, "total_tokens": 339}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{D}\\). This option accurately reflects the original arguments: it indicates that either Nancy or Marvin is at work, clarifies the conditional relationship regarding lawyers and dinner, asserts Nancy's status, and properly concludes that not everything is at work. The phrasing aligns well with predicate logic interpretations.", "ground_truth": "0", "score": 0.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 8.01e-05}}
{"global_index": "MMLU_formal_logic_25", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether each set of propositions is consistent. If the set is consistent, choose an option with a consistent valuation. (There may be other consistent valuations.)\nD ≡ E\nF ∨ D\n~E · ~F\n\nOptions: \nA. Inconsistent\nB. Consistent. Consistent valuation when D, E, and F are true\nC. Consistent. Consistent valuation when D and E are true and F is false\nD. Consistent. Consistent valuation when D and F are true and E is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "To determine the consistency of the propositions, we can analyze the implications of each statement. The propositions state that D is equivalent to E (D ≡ E), F is true or D is true (F ∨ D), and both E and F must be false (~E · ~F). For the set to be consistent, we notice that if D and E are both true, F must be false, which creates a contradiction with our valuations. The only consistent valuation is when D is true, E is true, and F is false, aligning with option C.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\boxed{C}.", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 179, "output_tokens": 125, "total_tokens": 304}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "To determine the consistency of the propositions, we can analyze the implications of each statement. The propositions state that D is equivalent to E (D ≡ E), F is true or D is true (F ∨ D), and both E and F must be false (~E · ~F). For the set to be consistent, we notice that if D and E are both true, F must be false, which creates a contradiction with our valuations. The only consistent valuation is when D is true, E is true, and F is false, aligning with option C.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\boxed{C}.", "ground_truth": "0", "score": 0.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.00010185}}
{"global_index": "MMLU_formal_logic_99", "question": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Construct a complete truth table for the following pairs of propositions. Then, using the truth tables, determine whether the statements are logically equivalent or contradictory. If neither, determine whether they are consistent or inconsistent. Justify your answers.\n(G ∨ ~H) ⊃ G and ~G ≡ (~H · G)\n\nOptions: \nA. Logically equivalent\nB. Contradictory\nC. Neither logically equivalent nor contradictory, but consistent\nD. Inconsistent\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.", "llm_selected": "gpt-4o-mini", "generated_answer": "To analyze the propositions (G ∨ ~H) ⊃ G and ~G ≡ (~H · G), we can construct their truth tables. After evaluating all combinations of truth values for G and H, we find that the first statement is true in cases where G is true and valid in cases where G is false if H is also false. The second statement indicates that G and ~H must hold simultaneously for equivalence, which does not always occur. Therefore, the two propositions are neither logically equivalent nor contradictory, but they can be true at the same time under specific conditions, making them consistent. \n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{C}\\).", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 160, "output_tokens": 137, "total_tokens": 297}, "success": true, "provider": "openai", "error": null, "evaluation_result": {"extracted_answer": "To analyze the propositions (G ∨ ~H) ⊃ G and ~G ≡ (~H · G), we can construct their truth tables. After evaluating all combinations of truth values for G and H, we find that the first statement is true in cases where G is true and valid in cases where G is false if H is also false. The second statement indicates that G and ~H must hold simultaneously for equivalence, which does not always occur. Therefore, the two propositions are neither logically equivalent nor contradictory, but they can be true at the same time under specific conditions, making them consistent. \n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{C}\\).", "ground_truth": "2", "score": 1.0, "metric": "mcq_accuracy", "inference_cost": 0.00010619999999999999}}
